{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789762921,"nonce":"Uqh5G68qKFfZ8iSlXCpu8BvHeVI=","title":"binary_trees: the collector marks the long-lived tree once per 16 MB of allocation","message":"Python's binary_trees (depth 16) frees nothing itself: every node is reclaimed by the conservative collector, which marked the 12 MB long-lived tree 56 times at 5.6 ms each, 310 ms of a 620 ms run. The ZynML kernel releases each tree explicitly and runs in 250 ms exec. Measured with ZYNTAX_TRACE_GC=1 and ZYNTAX_DISABLE_GC=1 (370 ms, leaking 1 GB); growth 1/2/3/4 gave 0.68/0.48/0.41/0.38 s wall at 56/69/82/104 MB peak. The productive-collection budget is now twice the live set (ZYNTAX_GC_GROWTH=n overrides). What remains is structural: a nursery (b94b4cb) would let the short-lived trees die without marking the long-lived one, and a per-class release the drop analysis could insert for a provably unshared instance graph (the tree built by nested constructor calls and dead after check()) would free them the way the ZynML kernel does.","files":null}]}